Support Docker Compose devcontainers

Render and persist Compose models so pod lifecycle, reconnect, fork,
and recreate operate on isolated projects without depending on the
source files. Build the prepared agent image from its Compose service,
inject architecture-matched rumpel binaries into sidecars, and route
service-qualified port forwards directly into their target container.

Reuse Compose-built images across projects when their rendered build
configuration and local context contents are unchanged. Keep remote
contexts and secret or SSH inputs on Compose's normal build path because
their current contents cannot be represented by a local cache key.
Forward the invoking client's SSH agent to Compose builds that request
it, and keep pull, no-cache, and unresolved-argument builds off the
shortcut.

Migrate pre-Compose databases in place so existing pods and forwarded
ports remain available while new single-container and Compose pods use
the expanded metadata schema.

Keep projects safe to run concurrently by stripping published ports
and fixed container names, preserve project-relative networks and
volumes across forks, and follow the Compose overrideCommand default.
Honor Compose service dependencies when validating selected services,
and reject client-side bind mounts before targeting remote Docker hosts.
Repair tunnels, server routes, and sidecar forwards when Compose
containers are recreated outside rumpelpod without deadlocking pod
lifecycle recovery.
